<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ram Prasad Bismil</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1897">1897</a>–<a href="/years/1927">1927</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Bismil, Pandit Ram Prasad Bismil</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ram Prasad Bismil was an Indian poet, writer and revolutionary against British rule who wrote in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> and Hindi under the pen names Ram, Agyat and Bismil. Born in Shahjahanpur district in the North-Western Provinces, he formed the revolutionary group Matrivedi and took part in the Mainpuri Conspiracy of 1918, then lived in hiding. A founding member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindustan">Hindustan</a> Republican Association, he planned the Kakori train robbery of 9 August 1925, in which ten revolutionaries stopped a train near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucknow">Lucknow</a> to seize the government treasury it carried. After 18 months of legal process he was sentenced to death and hanged at Gorakhpur Jail on 19 December 1927.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ram-prasad-bismil.jpg" alt="Ram Prasad Bismil" width="183" height="244" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Krantmlverma at Hindi Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Accused_of_Cacori_Conspiracy1271.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
